//? Title: Rayleigh quotient iteration: actual changing shifts, robust directions and residual stopping
//? Subtitle: Actual canonical histories, retained original fixtures and explicit controls
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
An approximate eigenvector suggests its own shift through the Rayleigh quotient,
allowing inverse iteration to refine a nearby eigenpair rapidly. The native
producer now uses the canonical robust inverse solve, checks the actual
original-matrix residual before another solve, and returns matching
quotient/vector diagnostics. This corrects the overflow-normalized zero-vector
failures found in the preceding batch. The original rounded-basis three-by-three
input and starting direction remain.
)

\toc

> Mechanism and mathematical scope

For a nonzero direction x, compute its quotient and solve with that actual
changing shift:
$$ \mu=\frac{x^TAx}{x^Tx},\qquad (A-\mu I)y=x,\qquad x^+=y/\|y\|. $$
\alttext(The actual Rayleigh quotient supplies the next inverse-system shift, followed by robust normalization.)
For symmetric matrices near a simple eigenvector under the usual nondegeneracy
conditions, the quotient error is quadratic in angular error and the inverse
step yields local cubic convergence. This is a local statement, not a guarantee
of the eigenvalue nearest the initial quotient. A balanced start can even
produce an exactly singular shift before convergence.

The canonical inverse kernel uses widened partial-pivot LU and hypot-based
normalization. Relative stopping uses the actual residual divided
by(Frobenius(A)+abs(mu))*norm(x). A converged state stops before an unnecessary
near-singular solve; an exact singular nonconverged shift throws instead of
regularizing into a false zero direction. An exhausted finite budget returns
converged=false with the actual matching pair. Dense changing shifts require a
new cubic-cost factorization each solve.

